{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310780,"nonce":"LLwuochKZm0oLpqsAuUTTX51yYI=","title":"MEASURED: the embedded stdlib .bsym manifest makes cold compile ~40% SLOWER, because it adds symbol registration while removing no work","message":"MEASURED 2026-08-15 at d09c1f29, interleaved 4x, tight variance, nbody cold\n(`rm -rf .rayzor` each run). Manifest disabled by planting a garbage\n`.rayzor/blade/stdlib/stdlib.bsym` so `load_symbol_manifest` fails and\n`bsym_loaded` goes false (compilation.rs:1680-1689 prefers the on-disk file\nover the embedded one — that local override is also a SILENT behaviour fork).\n\n| cold nbody | stdlib | tc.imports | tc.user | compile |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| manifest ON  | 6.1ms | 12ms | 11ms | **31ms** |\n| manifest OFF | 1.5ms | 8.0ms | 7.6ms | **18ms** |\n\nSame compile both ways (shake 93 -\u003e 32 identically). **The manifest costs\n~13ms and is slower in ALL THREE phases**, not just its own.\n\n**CORRECTION — .bsym IS NOT \"SYMBOLS ONLY\". It carries FULL POST-TYPECHECK\nDECLARATION METADATA** for all 226 modules, and I repeatedly claimed otherwise\nby quoting the comment at compilation.rs:919-932 instead of reading the structs.\n`BladeClassInfo` (blade.rs:561) has extends/implements/type_params/fields/\nmethods/static_fields/static_methods/constructor/native_name; `BladeMethodInfo`\n(:535) has params (each with type, optional, default), return_type, is_static,\nis_inline, type_params; `BladeFieldInfo` (:507) has field_type; `BladeEnumInfo`\nvariants with params+ordinals; `BladeAbstractInfo` underlying/from/to/forward.\nTypes are stored AS STRINGS.\n\nCONSEQUENCES:\n- The 12-file re-parse at compilation.rs:919-932 is REDUNDANT WORK, not a data\n  gap: `StaticSigIndex` wants `parser::Type` values, .bsym stores type STRINGS.\n  A REPRESENTATION MISMATCH. Fix = populate the sig index FROM the manifest\n  (parse a type STRING, not a whole FILE), never add a second section.\n- **NO BOOTSTRAP PROBLEM for build.rs generation.** `extract_type_info_from_ast`\n  (preblade.rs:418) builds all of `BladeTypeInfo` from the RAW PARSER AST — no\n  typechecker — because Haxe declarations carry explicit annotations. So .bsym\n  is producible from `parser` + the schema types alone; build.rs can generate it\n  with `parser` as a [build-dependencies]. The \"needs the LLVM-linked compiler,\n  so staged build or xtask\" concern is WRONG.\n\nWHY IT IS STILL A NET LOSS: the artifact ADDS work and REMOVES none, because\nnothing consumes the declarations it already carries:\n- `load_stdlib` STILL parses all 12 default imports anyway, purely to feed the\n  static-signature index — the code says so at compilation.rs:919-932.\n- `load_imports_efficiently` still resolves to real files\n  (`resolve_qualified_path_to_file_force`), so import COUNT is unchanged with\n  and without the manifest.\n- Registration is O(all 226 modules) regardless of what the program uses.\nSo it is pure overhead today. **Growing the manifest makes cold start worse.**\n\nThis INVERTS the older note (manifest 18ms vs source 53ms) — that ordering no\nlonger holds at HEAD. Re-measure; do not trust the old number.\n\nDEFAULT IMPORTS ARE 12, not 6 (compilation.rs:533-548): StdTypes, String,\nArray, Math, Std, Type, rayzor/concurrent/{Thread,Channel,Mutex,Arc},\nhaxe/iterators/{ArrayIterator,ArrayKeyValueIterator}. Every cold compile of\nevery program lowers the four CONCURRENCY modules even if it never threads —\nan independent lever.\n\nTO MAKE THE ARTIFACT PAY, it must replace `tc.imports`, not sit beside it.\nCeiling is real: warm `.blade` (cache_hit=7) drops tc.imports 14ms -\u003e 1.1-2.5ms,\nand the EXISTING restore path (`try_load_import_from_cache` -\u003e\n`register_symbols_from_type_info` -\u003e `renumber_and_push_import_mir`) already\nproduces correct output. So the work is not a new mechanism; it is making that\nmechanism run from a shipped, program-INDEPENDENT artifact installed at setup.\n\n**BLADE IS NOT THE SNAPSHOT FORMAT — OWNER RULING, do not re-litigate.** BLADE is\nan INCREMENTAL cache: it exists to make the NEXT compile of the SAME program\nfast. `hash_source_for_config` (compilation.rs:1063-1097) deliberately folds\n`user_program_hash()` into every module's key, and the comment says why — a\ncached module carries state assigned relative to the WHOLE program (id\nrenumbering, class layout, reflection ctor wrappers, inherited fields), so\ncross-program reuse was UNSOUND (`Cannot find name 'root'`, `__reflect_ctor_wrap`\nW0020, load SIGSEGV). That key is the design working, NOT a defect to normalize\naway. Do not try to make .blade payloads program-independent, and do not pickle\n.blade entries into the binary.\n\nMEASURED CONSEQUENCE (and the real size of the prize): compile program A cold\n(cache_miss=7), re-run A (fully cached, `jit 6µs`), then compile program B **in\nthe same directory** — B gets `cache_hit=0 cache_miss=7`. **The cache never\nhelps a NEW program.** Every distinct program pays the full stdlib import cost\non every machine, forever. So the snapshot's win is not \"the first run\"; it is\nEVERY program, EVERY time.\n\nSNAPSHOT DESIGN THAT FOLLOWS: store the stdlib lowered ONCE, PRE-MERGE, in its\nown stable self-contained id space with FQN-keyed cross-module references —\nprogram-independent BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by scrubbing a cache entry. The\nper-program merge/renumber then runs at install (O(instructions), the ~1.5ms\nmerge floor already measured) instead of re-doing parse -\u003e typecheck -\u003e HIR -\u003e\nMIR for 12 modules (~12.5ms).\n\nDEAD END — DO NOT BUILD THE SIG-INDEX SECTION. Serializing `StaticSigIndex`\n(compiler/src/tast/sig_index.rs) to kill the re-parse at compilation.rs:919-932\nlooks attractive (pure FQN-keyed data, no id space, program-independent for\nfree) but is worth **~1ms of a 31ms compile**. Proof: the manifest-OFF branch\n(compilation.rs:933-942) loads AND pre-registers all 12 default files and the\nWHOLE branch costs 1.5ms. So parsing the defaults is ≲1.5ms, and the manifest's\nextra 4.6ms in the stdlib phase is **registering 226 modules**, not parsing.\n(TRAP: don't try to time this with `rayzor check` — ~30ms process startup\nswamps sub-ms parse costs and yields negative deltas.)\n\nOPEN QUESTION, now on the critical path: WHY does manifest registration make\nLATER phases slower — tc.imports 8→12ms and tc.user 7.6→11ms? Registering\nsymbols should not cost time downstream. Suspects: more root-scope candidates\nto scan; `mark_file_loaded` altering import resolution; the stdlib merge walking\nmore entries (`build_combined_mapping`, `seed_property_accessors_from_typed_file`\nover every loaded TypedFile, `id_replacements` over the merged module). This\nmatters because a real snapshot registers MORE up front — if registration\ninherently taxes downstream phases, a bigger artifact makes it worse. The tax is\nonly acceptable if the snapshot REPLACES the import work (imports collapse to\n~0) rather than adding to it. ANSWER THIS BEFORE BUILDING THE MIR SECTION.\n\nDIAGNOSTIC BUILT: `scripts/stdlib_artifact_determinism.sh` compiles two\ndifferent programs and byte-compares the artifacts they share. Useful for\nenumerating what program-relative state lowering carries (that is how the list\nabove was confirmed). It is NOT a gate on .blade, which is legitimately\nprogram-keyed.\n\nBYTE-LEVEL FINDINGS from that diagnostic (why \"2158 differing bytes\" was\nmisleading): the divergence is dominated by BladeMetadata bookkeeping —\n`compile_timestamp`/`source_timestamp` (wall clock; decoded 1786792780, B one\nsecond later) and `source_hash` — and ONE varint width change there cascades\nthrough the whole postcard payload (postcard is positional). Realigning by that\nsingle byte drops Array from 2158 differing bytes to **11**. Always test the\ncascade hypothesis before reporting a byte count. String sets were IDENTICAL\nbetween the two programs, so module CONTENT does not depend on which methods\nthe user program used.\n\nHISTORICAL — the lowered payload is CONTEXT-RELATIVE. Four leaks, all fixable at\ngeneration time: `IrFunction.symbol_id` (raw TAST SymbolId); `source_location.\nfile_id` (a per-run counter); `external_function_names` keyed by\n`import_base = 100_000 + import_mir_modules.len()*10_000` i.e. the producing\nrun's LOAD ORDER; and an `InternedString(NNNN)` that has leaked into a\nname-typed String field (unlocated — find before shipping).\nProof: same stdlib module compiled from two different programs differs\n(ArrayIterator 2360/2913 bytes differ; Math emitted for one program, not the\nother). ACCEPTANCE ORACLE: generate from two different driver programs and\nrequire BYTE-IDENTICAL output.\n\nKEY INSIGHT: a setup-phase install FIXES `import_base` by construction —\ninstall the default set first in manifest order and every module always lands\nat 100_000/110_000/120_000. So proactive install is not merely faster, it is\nthe PRECONDITION that makes the payload program-independent.\n\n`.bsym` VERSIONING IS A HAND-BUMPED `SYMBOL_VERSION: u32 = 1` (blade.rs:504),\nnever bumped despite struct changes, and validated only AFTER the whole\npostcard blob deserializes — so it cannot catch a layout skew. Meanwhile\n`.blade`, the per-machine CACHE, does carry the derived `RAYZOR_BUILD_ID`.\nThat is backwards: the SHIPPED artifact is the one that needs it.\nTRAP: `RAYZOR_BUILD_ID` is the WRONG identity for a checked-in asset — build.rs\nhashes ALL of `src/`, so it moves on every unrelated compiler edit and would\npermanently reject the asset. Derive `.bsym` identity over `haxe-std/` content\n+ the bsym schema types ONLY.\nAlso: `preblade` bakes ABSOLUTE build-machine paths (226 `/Users/amaterasu/...`\nhits in the committed bytes), and per-module `source_hash` hashes the file\nPATH, not its contents, and is never compared on load.\n\nSee [[bugs_bsym_manifest_binds_bare_names_shadowing_user_types]] (the\nshadowing bug this artifact caused, fixed at d09c1f29) and\n[[feedback_fqn_first_resolution]].\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_bsym_manifest_is_net_negative_on_cold_start` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}